-- title:
--   Genus–cusp–elliptic-point count for X₁(M) over κ
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, let $M$ be a nonzero natural number with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$, and let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$. Write $F =$ [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldC`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L134) $\kappa\,M$, the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $\kappa((q))$ obtained by adjoining to $\kappa$ the ratios of integral forms of level $\Gamma_1(M)$, and let $g =$ [`AlgebraicCurve.genusFF`](def/AlgebraicCurve_Repartitions.html#L145) $\kappa\,F$, defined as the $\kappa$-dimension of $H^1$ of the zero divisor of $F/\kappa$. Let $N_3$, $N_2$ and $c$ be the cardinalities of the double coset spaces of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ by the subsets $\Gamma_1(M)$ on the left and, on the right, the cyclic group generated by $ST$, the cyclic group generated by $S$, and the subgroup generated by $T$ together with $-1$, respectively; let $n$ be the index in $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ of the subgroup generated by $\Gamma_1(M)$ and $-1$. The assertion is the identity $$2g + N_3 + N_2 + c = n + 2.$$
--
--   This is the Riemann–Hurwitz count for the covering $X_1(M) \to X(1)$, with the ramification data recorded as purely group-theoretic double coset counts over the elliptic points of order $3$ and $2$ and over the cusps, transported to an algebraically closed field $\kappa$ of characteristic $p \nmid M$, where the genus is unchanged. It feeds the later analysis of places and orders of vanishing on the level-$\Gamma_1(M)$ function field over $\kappa$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_two_mul_genusFF_x1FunctionFieldC_add_natCard_doubleCoset_eq_index_add_two.lean

import Mathlib
